==English==

===Etymology===
From late {{inh|en|enm|absolutly}},<ref>{{R:MED|id=MED218|entry=absō̆lūtlī|pos=adv}}</ref> equivalent to {{suf|en|absolute|ly}}.
{{etydate|1425|ref={{R:OED Online|pos=adv}}}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈæb.səˌl(j)uːt.li/|/ˌæb.səˈl(j)uːt.li/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˌæb.səˈlut.li/|/ˈæb.səˌlut.li/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-absolutely.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# In an absolute or [[unconditional]] manner; [[utterly]], [[positively]], [[wholly]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:completely}}
# [[independently|Independently]]; viewed without relation to other things or factors.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:individually}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Introduction to Arithmetic|passage=Another characteristic of numbers, which does not concern their mutual relations so much as themselves '''absolutely''', is their ability, according to the ideas of Nicomachus, to conform to geometrical arrangements.|year=1926|author=Nicomachus of Gerasa|page=50|authorlink=Nicomachus|translator=Martin Luther D'Ooge|pageurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Introduction_to_Arithmetic/wdhMAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA50chapter=III|chapterurl=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Introduction_to_Arithmetic/wdhMAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&pg=PA46|publisher=w:The Macmillan Company|location=New York|url=https://www.google.ca/books/edition/Introduction_to_Arithmetic/wdhMAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=0|author2=Frank Egleston Robbins|authorlink2=Frank Egleston Robbins|author3=Louis Charles Karpinski|authorlink3=Louis Charles Karpinski}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Absolutely'' is not to be confused with [[intensive]]s such as ''very'' or ''indeed'', as it is an unconditional term, ''absolutely/*very essential, vital or crucial''.

